  - traceDigest_ reads t.mz/rt/intensity — accessors must return bit-identical doubles. mz accessor = mzOf(tof, b[apex]) — but today, for the integer path, t.mz = cmz (the weighted centroid, line 950!) NOT mzOf(tof, b). Hold on — line 950: `t.mz = cmz; // the weighted centroid, as OpenMS reports`. And the accessor table in the doc says mz = tofAxis().mzOf(tof, sl.b[frame0+apex]). Those are DIFFERENT values! The integer detector sets t.mz = cmz (running weighted centroid), while tof/b are the apex peak's. Where is t.mz consumed? (a) the frag canonical sort (im, mz, rt, intensity) — sort ORDER could change if mz changes → fragment indices fi change → does anything depend on fi order? The scorer iterates fi from lower_bound over frag_im; emit order affects bucket order but canonical sort at the end fixes spectra order. BUT: assembleFromList_ ties break on m/z then index a<b — index! partial_sort tie-break uses index a (frag position in the vector). If sort order of frag_traces changes, indices change, tie-breaks differ → output can differ when scores and m/z tie. Rare but possible. (b) `fabs(f.mz - pc.mono_mz) < 0.01` precursor-peak exclusion (line 2148) and drop_prec_iso (line 2154): mz used in a 0.01 Da gate. cmz vs mzOf(apex bin) differ by sub-bin amounts — could flip a borderline exclusion → digest change. (c) exportMz_ prefers tof/b anyway, so exported m/z unchanged. (d) traceDigest_ includes t.mz → digest changes by construction!

  Wait — but the digest in question is "the spectrum digest of the integer path" — of emitted spectra presumably, not traceDigest_. Emitted spectra's precursor m/z comes from pc.mono_tof/mono_b (unchanged) and fragments from exportMz_(tof, b, mz) — cached mz only used when tof==0. So emitted m/z values unchanged even if the cached mz changes. But the [M-7] precursor-exclusion gate `fabs(f.mz - pc.mono_mz) < 0.01` uses the cached f.mz! If f.mz changes from cmz to mzOf(tof,b), a fragment at the boundary flips. Also drop_prec_iso gate. Also the canonical frag sort (a.mz != b.mz) — order changes possible. And inferPrecursors_ for MS1: by_mz sorted on ms1[a].mz; findPartner windows use s.mz ± tol; mono selection compares ms1[j].mz == best_mono; MS1 mz accessor change → partner selection could change → precursor set changes → digest change. For MS1 traces, what is t.mz today? MS1 traces go through detectTraces_ (OpenMS path always — MS1 uses MassTraceDetection, line 2628) → toTrace → t.mz = apex peak m/z (or centroid/median by estimator). With estimator=apex: t.mz = apex peak's getMZ() — the PICKED m/z (double from the picked spectrum). The redesign: mz = mzOf(tof, b) where tof = apex bin... For the OPENMS path, tof = 0 (no bin) — so accessor returns what? The doc says "mz: tofAxis().mzOf(tof, sl.b[frame0+apex])". For OpenMS-detector traces, tof==0 and the old mz must be RETAINED. But the new struct has no mz field! The doc's risk table doesn't address this: for the OpenMS detector, the picked m/z of the apex peak is NOT derivable from a bin. The picked m/z came from the compact store's mzq (1e-5 Da quantum) → toSlab inverts to bin → so for the integer path, tof is known and mzOf(tof,b) ≈ but NOT == mzq/MZ_Q (round-trip through calibration, plus quantization to bin!). Wait — in the integer path, what is the relationship between t.mz (cmz) and mzq? cmz is a weighted centroid of mzOf(tof_k, b_frame) values. And for toTrace in the OpenMS path (MS1 and OpenMS MS2), peaks carry picked m/z (from mzq, quantized 1e-5), and there's no tof at all. So the redesign must either (a) keep a cached mz double for OpenMS-path traces (field needed — struct grows), or (b) recompute mz = mzOf(tofOf(mzq/MZ_Q...)) which CHANGES the value (double quantization: 1e-5 Da quantum → bin → calibrated back). The picked m/z would change → isotope partner matching, precursor m/z, sort orders all shift slightly → digest changes AND real output changes. So the claim "the doubles are caches of things a few instructions recompute" is FALSE for the OpenMS path's mz, and even for the integer path's cached mz=cmz (centroid, not apex-bin calibration).

  Hold on, let me double check what the integer path does with t.mz downstream... yes as analyzed: gates and sorts. So the accessor table's claim that mz is derivable is wrong in two ways:
  1. integer path: cached mz = weighted centroid cmz — NOT mzOf(tof, b). The doc's own accessor would change gate inputs.
  2. openms path: mz = picked apex m/z, no bin exists; irreducible.

  Unless... the doc intends mz accessor to only be used "at gate/export time" and accepts value changes? No — it claims pure representation with identical digest.

  Also `im`: today t.im = IM_LO + cim/IM_Q where cim is the weighted IM centroid (integer path, line 952) — a double centroid of quantized values divided... wait cim is in imq units (double running centroid), so t.im = IM_LO + cim/IM_Q with cim fractional. The redesign stores imq as uint16 quantized centroid: IM_LO + imq/IM_Q loses the fractional part → t.im changes → frag_im sort order, IM gates (fabs(frag_im - pc.im) at boundaries), findPartner IM gates, dedup, etc. all shift by up to half a quantum (2.1e-5/2). Borderline gate flips possible → digest change. And im accessor "one multiply" loses precision. Same for MS1: t.im = mt.getCentroidIM() (OpenMS, double from picked IM floats) — quantizing to uint16 loses precision (quantum 2.1e-5 vs delta_im gate 0.01 — 500x margin, but borderline cases exist). Hmm, but the compact store ALREADY quantizes IM to uint16 for peaks (line 607). But trace centroids are computed as weighted means of those quantized values and stored as doubles — the centroid of quantized values has more precision than a quantum. So yes, imq quantization of the centroid is lossy. Digest risk.

  And `rt`: today t.rt = rtAxis()[xi[ap]] (integer) or mt.getCentroidRT() (openms!). OpenMS path rt = centroid RT — NOT any frame's RT! Line 307: t.rt = mt.getCentroidRT(). Redesign: rt = rtAxis()[rt_index[frame0+apex]] — the apex FRAME's RT. Different value! Used in: pc.rt → gate RT comparisons (fabs(frag_rt - pc.rt) > delta_rt), findPartner RT gates, emitted spectrum RT (out.setRT(pc.rt), line 2336!), merge/consolidate windows, canonical sort. So for the OpenMS path the reported spectrum RT would change — a real output change, not representation. For the integer path rt = apex frame RT already. The doc says both detectors produce the SAME structure — but it changes OpenMS-path rt semantics. Since the falsifier is about the INTEGER path digest, and the integer path's rt IS apex frame RT... OK for integer. But the redesign also touches the OpenMS path (toTrace) — and changes its output. Doc should scope the change to the integer path or note the OpenMS path changes.

  Similarly `intensity`: integer path t.intensity = t.xv[ap] = arena[off+apex] ✓. OpenMS: mt.getMaxIntensity(false) ✓ same. OK.

  `b`: integer: sl.b[apex frame] ✓. openms: b=0 — redesign: b accessor sl.b[frame0+apex] would return nonzero for openms traces → exportMz_(tof=0, b≠0, cached) → tof==0 → returns cached. OK fine.

  Now the `mz` for the integer path — let me recheck: maybe I should check what consumes t.mz for integer-path fragments: [M-7] gate fabs(f.mz - pc.mono_mz) < 0.01. f.mz=cmz (centroid), new: mzOf(apex tof, b). Difference up to ~half bin ≈ m/z-dependent (bin ≈ 1e-4..1e-3 Da?). TOF bin in m/z at 600: tofToMz — a bin is maybe ~0.0005-0.002 Da. Centroid vs apex within a few bins → < 0.01 mostly, but gate flips at the boundary in either case; count of flips tiny but nonzero → digest change possible. Also the frag sort by mz.

  OK. Now question 4: arena and task pool. Sequence today: band tasks (taskloop) produce per-band vector<Trace> per[b]; concatenated serially after taskloop joins (implicit barrier at end of taskloop? A taskloop inside a task — the encountering task waits at taskloop end, yes there's an implicit taskgroup). Then splitIntegerTraces (chunked taskloop). Then buildFragGrid taskloop. Under the redesign: each band task appends to its OWN arena; the window concatenates after band tasks join, rebasing off. Hazards:
  (a) The band taskloop and concatenation: the concatenation must happen after the taskloop completes — natural (implicit barrier). But note the band tasks run inside a window TASK in the shared task pool; other windows' tasks interleave. Each band's arena is private → safe. Concatenation serial in the window task → safe.
  (b) EPD chunked task loop runs AFTER concatenation (splitIntegerTraces is called after frag_traces concatenated) — so children sub-spans reference the REBASED arena. But wait — if children are sub-spans of the parent's arena range, and the parent's arena entries were built pre-split, then after split the parents' traces are discarded but their arena ranges must be RETAINED (children reference them). The arena can only grow; no compaction. Fine. But: the split taskloop appends NEW child traces — do children need NEW arena space? Doc says no copy: child = sub-span. But the parent trace had points beyond the child (other children) — no extra storage. OK. However splitIntegerTraces currently FREES parent profiles chunk-wise (t.freeProfile()) to shave peak memory — with arena no-copy children, parent ranges stay live until window end. Memory: total arena = sum over parents of spans + nothing extra. Same as before. Fine.
  (c) The scorer's tasks (taskloop over precursors, grainsize 32) index the arena — read-only at that point. The arena must not grow during scoring. Does anything grow it? No — traces are final by then. But there's the rebase: "the window concatenates and rebases offsets" — the rebase writes to every trace's `off`. Who reads `off` before the rebase? Within the window: splitIntegerTraces runs after concatenation+rebase (order: bands → concat → split → grid/score). If the rebase happens at concat time, all later stages see final off. But do band tasks READ off of other bands' traces? No. Does splitIntegerTraces' chunked taskloop read off while another chunk... all post-rebase. One more: the arena concatenation itself — band arenas are vectors held in per[b] traces + separate arena vectors; concatenation is serial. Safe sequencing: (1) band taskloop joins; (2) serial concat + rebase; (3) split taskloop (reads arena, appends child Trace structs — children share parent ranges, no arena writes; but the TRACE vector grows: parts[ci] private per chunk, concatenated after join — as today); (4) scorer read-only. The hazard the doc must name: if children were instead COPIED into fresh arena space during the split taskloop (to allow freeing parent ranges), appends from concurrent chunks to the shared window arena would race and reallocation would... offsets are indices, so reallocation is safe for validity, but concurrent push_back on one vector races. The doc's no-copy design avoids this. Also: who reads `off` before rebase — the band's own traces are read by NO ONE between band task and concat... except: detOn_() digest? traceDigest_ reads mz/rt/intensity — intensity accessor reads arena[off+apex] — if the digest is computed per band before concat, it reads the band-private arena with un-rebased off — consistent within the band. OK.

  But there IS a real hazard: EPD's chunked task loop in splitIntegerTraces currently FREES each parent's profile chunk-wise (memSub, freeProfile) BEFORE other chunks have run. In the redesign, if children are sub-spans, freeing the parent's arena range is impossible anyway (arena is append-only). Fine.

  Another hazard: with dense spans, splitIntegerTraces builds MassTrace from (rt_index, arena) — it must SKIP zeros when building the MassTrace (EPD must not see zero points — today it sees only real points; zeros would change EPD's smoothing and extrema → different splits!). Actually would zeros change EPD? EPD smooths the profile with SG filter over the point sequence; inserting zeros at gaps changes the sequence (adds points) → different smoothing → different split points → different traces → digest change. So the MassTrace fed to EPD must contain only nonzero points. The doc says "builds its MassTrace from (rt_index, arena)" — must specify zero-skipping. Then children come back as MassTrace slices of the nonzero point list; mapping child back to (frame0+a, len): child points are a subset of parent points; the child span = [first point's window-local frame, last point's frame] with zeros for internal gaps. Since child points ⊂ parent points, child span ⊆ parent span → sub-span of parent's arena range ✓ (zeros align by frame). Good — but requires per-point frame mapping during conversion (child's first point → which parent position?). Can be computed: child point RTs are exact frame RTs; first child point's window-local frame = parent's (frame0 + index of that point in parent's nonzero sequence...) — need the parent's point frames, which are frame0+i for nonzero positions. Doable by matching intensity+RT. OK but flag the zero-skip requirement for EPD input — the doc doesn't mention it, and it's exactly the class of thing that silently changes the digest.

  Question 5: arithmetic. Today: 120 B struct + 3 malloc headers + 12 B/point. Measured 10.7 points/trace. Doc: 120 + 48 + 12×10.7 = 296. Hmm 120+48=168, +128.4 = 296.4 ✓ (48 = 3 malloc headers × 16 B?). Redesign: 18 + 4×14 = 74 with "call it 14 spanned frames". Is 14 right given the sample-rate rule? Sample rate: size/(frames visited - trailing misses) >= 0.5. Frames visited - trailing misses ≈ span + leading/interior misses... Let me think: total = dn.scans + up.scans + 1 (frames visited, including the seed frame and misses up to 5+1 each side? direction stops after >5 consecutive misses, i.e., up to 6 misses each side). adjusted = total - dn.missed - up.missed. dn.missed counts CONSECUTIVE misses at termination? No — d.missed is reset to 0 on each hit (++d.hits; d.missed = 0). So at the end, dn.missed = trailing misses only (misses since last hit). adjusted = visited frames minus TRAILING misses = frames from first hit to last hit + 1 = the span (fmin..fmax inclusive), approx. Actually: total counts every frame step taken (scans) plus 1; subtracting trailing misses leaves frames from the first accepted... hmm, leading misses: the walk starts at the seed (a hit) so there are no leading misses. Interior misses are included in adjusted. So adjusted ≈ span length (frames from fmin to fmax inclusive). The rule: size/adjusted >= 0.5 → span <= 2 × size. So spanned frames <= 2 × real points. With 10.7 points: worst span = 21.4, i.e. at most 2x padding ✓ the doc's bound is right: "at most half the entries are padding". But the doc says "call it 14 spanned frames" as the TYPICAL case: 10.7 real + gaps → 14 implies ~30% gaps. My own gap estimate: sample rate 0.5 floor; typical accepted traces have sample rate well above 0.5 (say 0.7-0.9), so span ≈ 10.7/0.75 ≈ 14.3 — consistent. But the doc's arithmetic "worst case is 8 B per real point" ✓. Corrected numbers: today 120 + 48 + 12×10.7 = 296 B/trace. New: 18 + 4×span. Worst case span = 21.4 → 18 + 85.6 = 103.6 B/trace (not 74). Typical 74. Either way < 296. But ALSO: vector capacity overhead — today's xi/xv/xt vectors may over-allocate (capacity ≥ size, typically up to 2x on growth) — the measured 35.6 GB includes capacity slack, so actual improvement may be better. Also malloc headers: 3 allocations × 16 B = 48. Fine.

  But wait — the 2x bound uses adjusted = total - dn.missed - up.missed where misses are trailing only... but what about a trace that terminated one direction early on a "taken" miss? d.missed counts taken-as-miss too. At loop end, dn.missed = misses after the last hit in that direction (all trailing) since reset on hits. Yes. But: a direction also stops when it runs out of frames (edge of map) — no misses counted, fine. So adjusted ≥ span? Let me verify: total = dn.scans + up.scans + 1. scans counts every step the direction moved, including steps into empty frames (not counted as misses — empty frames are skipped without ++missed but ++scans happens). Hmm: an empty frame (no members) is not a miss (per OpenMS semantics, line 899-903: only if frame_live/fails-empty check then best()... missed increments only when frame is live but no peak accepted). So frames VISITED includes empty frames (not misses). adjusted = total - trailing misses includes interior EMPTY frames. The span (fmin..fmax) also includes interior empty frames. Leading? The seed is a hit, directions move out; the first step down may be an empty frame — it's counted in scans and in adjusted, and it lies within [fmin,fmax]? No! If the frame below the seed is empty, and the next frame has a hit, then fmin is the hit's frame — the empty frame is inside the span. But what about empty frames beyond the last hit but before the misses that terminated? E.g., hits at seed going down to frame fmin; between the last hit and termination there can be up to 6 consecutive misses (counted, subtracted) — but EMPTY frames interleaved among those trailing misses are counted in scans and NOT subtracted (not misses) yet are OUTSIDE the span [fmin, fmax]. So adjusted can EXCEED the span by trailing empty frames. Then size/adjusted >= 0.5 does NOT imply span <= 2×size; span <= adjusted×... hmm: adjusted = span + trailing_empty. size/span could be < 0.5 while size/adjusted >= 0.5? No: adjusted ≥ span → size/adjusted ≤ size/span. Rule: size/adjusted ≥ 0.5 → size/span ≥ size/adjusted ≥ 0.5 → span ≤ 2×size. The implication holds. ✓ Bound stands: span ≤ 2×size. But wait — min_sample_rate applies per trace at acceptance; ms2_msr default -1 → 0.5. But trace:ms2_min_sample_rate can be set lower (0.3, 0.1 tested!). With msr = 0.1, span ≤ 10×size → 18 + 40×10.7 = 446 B/trace worst case — WORSE than today. The doc's "at most 2x" is conditional on msr = 0.5. Also OpenMS path: min_sample_rate parameter passed similarly; MS1 min 0.5, MS2 -1→0.5. But the parameter exists; the doc should bound by the CONFIGURED msr: padding ≤ (1/msr - 1) × 4 B per real point. Corrected worst case at default 0.5: 103.6 B/trace; at msr 0.1: 446 B/trace.

  Hmm wait, also EPD split children: children are sub-spans of parents; child span includes the parent's internal gaps AND the child's own — child span/points can be worse than 2x? Child points ⊂ parent points, child span ⊆ parent span. A child with 3 points spanning the parent's full span? EPD splits at valleys — children typically span less. But worst case a child could have span >> 2×its points (e.g., parent 30 points over span 30, child with points {0, 15, 29} → span 30, 10x). EPD children aren't subject to the sample-rate rule! So per-trace storage after splitting can exceed the 2x bound. Measured 10.7 points/trace is presumably POST-split. The post-split span distribution isn't bounded by msr. Flag it: the 4 B/spanned-frame cost is bounded by the PARENT's msr only for unsplit traces; children inherit parent gaps and can have arbitrarily bad density in the worst case (though bounded by parent span). Aggregate storage: sum of child spans ≤ sum of parent spans? Children partition... EPD children of one parent: spans are sub-intervals [a_i, b_i] of the parent's span, possibly overlapping at boundaries by one point, and not covering gaps between... if children tile the parent span disjointly, sum of child spans ≈ parent span + (#children - 1) boundary overlaps. If EPD drops points between children (valley points excluded), sum < parent span. And in the redesign children don't copy — they reference the PARENT's arena range! So arena storage = parent spans only, regardless of children. The 4 B/spanned frame is paid per PARENT span once. Children are free. So the aggregate arena = sum over detected traces of span ≤ 2× total points (at msr 0.5). Fine — but only because of the no-copy child design; if children were copied, splitting would multiply arena bytes. Good.

  But one more subtlety on no-copy children: parent's arena range must remain; also the child needs zeros within its own sub-span at the parent's gap positions — already there. ✓. But child `off` = parent.off + a; len = b - a. apex = ap - a. ✓.

  Question 6: What else should go:
  - `off` as prefix sum: if traces are stored in arena order (each trace's intensities appended in trace order), off[i] = prefix sum of len — recoverable if you keep lengths: off[i] = sum len[0..i). But children are sub-spans, NOT appended — they break arena order. Also scorer reads traces by IM-sorted order (frag_im sort) — the sort permutes traces, breaking arena order correspondence unless the sort happens before arena layout. Sequence: detect → sort by im → build arena in sorted order → off = prefix sum computable from lens via scan. But then binary searches etc. need off; computing prefix sum on the fly is O(n) — no. You'd need an inclusive-scan array, which IS the off array. So dropping off buys 4 B but requires lens contiguous and an O(1) lookup structure — you could store len only and compute off via a scan at stage boundaries, but traces are accessed randomly (scorer by fi, ms1 by trace_idx) — a random access needs the prefix sum. Alternative: since children alias parents, arena-order storage fails anyway. Verdict: keep off for children; could drop for parents. Honest answer: off is needed for sub-span children and for random access after the IM sort; not worth deleting.
  - `imq` for MS2 traces: frag_im is used in the IM gate (binary search bounds + weighted_ dim) and sort. If dropped, gate uses... nothing else carries IM per trace. The precursor IM gate is fundamental (delta_im 0.01). Can't drop for MS2. For MS1: pc.im per precursor carries it, but isotope partner search uses ms1[j].im — needed. So no.
  - `apex`: used to compute rt, mz(via b), intensity, b — at gate/export time and sort time. apex is a scan of len floats — but it's needed at SORT time (frag canonical sort by rt/im/mz/intensity BEFORE arena? no, arena exists). A scan of ~11-21 floats per trace at sort and once per emit: cheap-ish but it makes every accessor O(len). Keep apex (2 B). Also the frag sort happens once; accessor calls per trace: sort (4 fields), scorer (intensity once per emit, mz per emit). Scan per access would be O(len) each — acceptable but pointless; 2 B is nothing. Keep.
  - What SHOULD go: the `mz`/`rt`/`im`/`intensity` cached doubles obviously (the point of the redesign) — but I showed mz/im/rt for the OpenMS path and integer path aren't all derivable bit-identically. So what I'd delete: xt (with the slab-lookup fix), xi (fine), and the malloc overhead. I'd NOT quantize im (keep a float im centroid? or store imq as uint32?) — precision issue.

  Question 7: the falsifier — byte-identical digest. FP evaluation order: 
  - buildFragGrid sum/sumsq over points in trace order — arena order same sequence (real points in ascending frame order, zeros skipped). If the scorer sums dot over len entries including zeros vs over np real points: additions of 0.0 exactly do not change FP sums (x + 0.0 == x for finite x, except -0/NaN). So skipping vs adding zeros: identical. BUT psum over touched: order same. dot: entries with arena==0 skipped or added as 0 — identical.
  - However: pdense accumulation `pdense[gi] += (float)pval` — with zero precursor points, adding 0.0f — no change. But touched logic (gi added with 0) changes var_support_ n — semantic change, not just order.
  - mean/invnorm: today computed in buildFragGrid over np real points; new: sum over len with zeros — same additions order? sum over arena range including zeros = same as sum over real points interleaved with +0.0 — bit-identical. ✓
  - The REAL digest breakers are the cached-value changes (mz=cmz vs accessor, im quantization, rt centroid for openms), G change, touched-inflation, FWHM/atrous zero semantics, xicCorr m change — these change gate DECISIONS, not just FP order. So byte-identical is achievable ONLY if all of those are fixed; any that can't be fixed bit-identically (e.g., im centroid quantization — fix: don't quantize, or store the double... but the struct has only imq uint16) breaks the gate. Options: make the digest gate apply to a config where... no — the gates (IM 0.01) are always on. im accessor IM_LO + imq/IM_Q vs IM_LO + cim/IM_Q: differs in the last ulps for every trace. fabs(frag_im - pc.im) > delta_im flips when |d| is within ~2e-5 of 0.01. Probability per comparison ~ 2e-5/typical-d-spread... with ~1e10 comparisons, flips WILL happen. So byte-identical is NOT achievable with imq quantization as specified. Correct gate: either (a) keep im as a double (struct grows by 4-8 B — store float im? float has 24-bit mantissa; im in [0.4,1.8], float ulp ~ 1e-7 — much better; cim/IM_Q double vs float rounding still differs but by ~1e-7, flip probability ~1e-7/0.01×density — still nonzero in 1e10 comparisons... hmm, but the compact store already quantizes PEAK im to uint16; the trace centroid is a mean of those. A float centroid of quantized values: the accessor value would need to equal the double running centroid — no.)
    So the honest position: byte-identical digest is achievable only if the accessor values equal today's cached values bit-for-bit, which requires: mz kept as double for openms path or... The struct can't hold them. Alternative correct gate: digest on the EMITTED SPECTRA with a tolerance? The project has fdrcheck / peptide-count gates. The doc itself says "the integer path is not gated on bit-identity... but THIS change should be". A pragmatic correct gate: (1) emitted-spectrum digest must match for the DEFAULT config where... no, it won't.
    Alternative: keep digest-identity as the gate but only after making accessors bit-identical: possible? For integer path: rt ✓ (apex frame RT), intensity ✓ (arena float → double, exact), b ✓, tof ✓, mz ✗ (cmz centroid vs anything derivable — cmz is a running iterative weighted mean with a specific update order! Not stored anywhere derivable. It's fundamentally a detector-state artifact. To keep mz=cmz you'd have to store it — 8 B — or accept the change).
    
    Wait — actually, let me reconsider: does the integer path NEED t.mz=cmz? What consumes it? The [M-7] gate, drop_prec_iso, frag canonical sort, and as exportMz_ fallback (tof≠0 so unused). If the redesign changes f.mz to mzOf(apex tof, b), the gate inputs change → borderline flips → digest changes (in the integer path itself, before/after). Unless the redesign also changes the gates to use the bin space (fabs in tof units) — but that's a semantic change too. So the doc's claim "pure representation, digest identical" FAILS on the integer path's own cached mz unless mz keeps cmz. Could store cmz... but that contradicts "mz derivable". 

    Hmm, wait — maybe I'm wrong that mz accessor replaces cmz. Doc table: `mz | tofAxis().mzOf(tof, sl.b[frame0 + apex])`. Yes it replaces.

    Actually also for MS1: t.mz from toTrace openms path = picked apex m/z. MS1 tracing stays OpenMS! (MS1 uses detectTraces_ always — line 2628 calls detectTraces_ regardless of integer_detector? Let me check: line 2628: ms1_traces = detectTraces_(ms1_map, ...) — yes, always OpenMS MTD.) So ALL MS1 traces have tof=0 and mz=picked value, rt=centroid, im=centroid. The redesign touches MS1 traces too (Precursor_ reads, inferPrecursors_, scorer precursor profiles, MS1 "PeakSlab-like frame table"). For MS1 there is no bin — the accessor can't reconstruct mz (mzOf(0, b) undefined), rt (centroid RT is between frames!), im. So for MS1 the new struct MUST keep doubles, or the redesign silently changes MS1 coordinates: pc.rt → spectrum RT, gate RT, precursor sort... rt = apex frame RT vs centroid RT differs by seconds. That's a REAL output change (spectrum RT moves). The doc's section 2 says "for MS1 traces in the MS1 map's sequence" — it does intend to convert MS1 traces to the new struct. And its accessor table claims rt = apex frame RT. Today MS1 t.rt = getCentroidRT(). NOT identical. → digest gate fails on MS1 immediately.

    Unless the falsifier is scoped to "integer path" MS2 only and MS1/openms keep old struct? The doc says "Both detectors then produce the SAME structure" — so no.

    Correct gate recommendation: byte-identical digest is achievable ONLY for a restricted definition, or after scoping: (i) accept accessor-exact fields (rt, intensity, b, tof for integer path) and budget the non-exact ones (mz, im, rt-centroid for openms) as deliberate, measured semantic changes with their own peptide-count A/B — i.e., the gate becomes: (a) emitted-spectrum digest identical when comparing old-cached vs new-cached for the fields that are exact; more practical: (b) run the SAME detector with the new struct and compare peptide counts at fixed FDR within the known run-to-run wobble (~0.7%), plus a digest on gate-invariant quantities (tof, b, rt_index, intensity profiles) which MUST be byte-identical. How tight: profile-level digest (frames + intensities) byte-identical; scalar-coordinate digest within stated quantization (im within half quantum, mz within the estimator difference); end-to-end peptides within noise.

  Alternatively, the fix that PRESERVES bit-identity: keep per-trace cached doubles for the three gate-facing scalars (mz, im, and rt where it's a centroid) — 24 B — still down from 120 + 3 mallocs. The doc's "all DERIVABLE" claim is what's wrong, not the arena.

