    2. touched inflation + n in var_support_ — fixable (skip zeros, carry real-point count).
    3. xicCorr m — fixable (skip zeros either side).
    4. atrousLevels/smoother input — fixable (nonzero subsequence), at the cost of keeping a nonzero-index structure for MS1 precursor traces.
    5. FWHM — fixable (nonzero walk).
    6. xt → child apex bin: fixable only with an EXACT reproduction of tofOf(mzOf(xt[ap], b), b) — requires xt[ap] from the parent at split time; a slab re-lookup can return a different bin in ties; and the round-trip must be replicated. Risky but doable if the parent hands the child the apex bin directly (child created by the splitter, which still has... wait, with xt deleted the splitter doesn't have xt[ap]. The splitter would look up the slab frame for the peak. Alternatively: keep per-point bins in a TRANSIENT arena that lives only until the split is done. That's honest: xt storage is needed through the split, then dropped. Peak memory during split includes it — the split is where EPD list memory dominates anyway.)
